# Architecture boundaries

- Start as a modular platform with independently deployable compute workers, not one microservice per department.
- Repository target:
  - `backend/`: Go control plane and business API.
  - `frontend/`: Next.js application at `/app`.
  - `ai-runtime/`: Python orchestration, RAG, extraction, and evaluation.
  - `model-gateway/`: internal OpenAI-compatible routing and model policy.
  - `workers/` or language-local worker entrypoints: durable background work.
  - `wordpress/`, `nginx/`, `deploy/`, `scripts/`.
- Go owns identity, authorization, tenancy, tool catalog, approvals, audit, retention, and integration policy.
- Python owns ML execution only. It must not become an independent source of authorization truth.
- Model servers never receive browser traffic directly.
- Dependency direction: HTTP handler → application service → repository/provider adapter.
- Domain logic must not live in handlers, React components, SQL migrations, or model prompts.
- Cross-service communication uses versioned HTTP contracts for queries and durable events/jobs for long work.
- PostgreSQL is the source of truth for business state; MinIO stores blobs; vector indexes are derived data.
- Every external provider, parser, model server, and enterprise system is behind an interface and registry.
- Unsloth Studio and Core may be forked, rewritten, and embedded in the holding portal. Studio is still not the identity system, job queue, or production gateway: Go keeps auth, tenancy, approvals, audit, and promotion; serving stays on the model gateway.
- Prefer a module inside the existing service until separate dependencies, scaling, blast radius, or release cadence justify extraction.
- Record a short ADR before adding a backend language, database, broker, vector engine, or orchestration framework.
- No direct imports across bounded-context internals; share explicit contracts and small common primitives only.
